      "text": "filename: docs/knife-sharpening.md\n---\n# How to Sharpen a Kitchen Knife by Hand\n\n## Gather Your Tools  \nBefore you begin, gather the necessary tools to ensure a safe and effective sharpening process. You will need a high-quality whetstone—preferably a natural or synthetic stone with a medium to fine grit, such as 800 or 1000 grit. A sharpening rod or a bench block provides stability and helps maintain the correct angle. A towel or cloth is essential for wiping the blade after sharpening to remove dust and debris. Additionally, have a pair of scissors or a knife block nearby to safely store your knife once sharpened. Make sure your work surface is clean, flat, and free of any vibrations. A magnifying glass or a small flashlight can help you inspect the edge for imperfections. Always use a knife with a straight edge and a consistent bevel to achieve the best results.\n\n## Sharpen the Edge  \nHold the knife at a consistent angle—typically between 15 and 20 degrees—using your dominant hand. This angle is crucial for creating a sharp, durable edge. Place the blade on the whetstone at a steady, even angle, and move the knife in a smooth, forward motion, applying light pressure. Use a back-and-forth motion, ensuring the entire edge is evenly sharpened. Focus on the bevel, the slanted edge of the blade, and avoid pressing too hard to prevent chipping or dulling. After a few strokes, switch to a finer grit whetstone to refine the edge and remove any burrs. Repeat the process until the blade feels smooth and cuts cleanly. Always maintain the same angle throughout to preserve the knife’s integrity and ensure a uniform edge.\n\n## Test and Store  \nAfter sharpening, test the knife by slicing through a piece of paper or a ripe tomato. A properly sharpened blade should cut through with minimal effort and a clean, smooth motion. If the knife still feels dull or struggles, repeat the sharpening process with more strokes or a finer grit stone. Once satisfied, wipe the blade with a dry cloth to remove any residue. Store the knife in a knife block, magnetic strip, or sheath to protect the edge and prevent damage. Regular maintenance with a whetstone and proper angle alignment will extend the life of your kitchen knife and keep it ready for daily use. Remember, a sharp knife is safer and more efficient than a dull one—always prioritize safety and precision.",
